# EMNLP long paper — four-role TODO collaboration rules

> **History**: Before 2026-04-19 the file was `three-role-todo-workflow.md` and covered only user / scientist / engineer; on 2026-04-19 the user clarified that the collaboration is actually **four-role** (with "reviewer" added). The file was renamed to `four-role-todo-workflow.md`, and on the same day the suffix was further changed to `.mdc` for Cursor rule-format compliance and the body was translated to English. Historical references to the old filename in `implementation_log.md` are preserved verbatim and **must not be retroactively rewritten**.

## 0. Role-to-state-file mapping

The repository contains **four persistent TODO files + one reviewer-archive directory family + one index**, each maintained by one of the four roles:

| Role | State / output file | Main content |
|------|---------------------|--------------|
| **User** (overall coordinator) | [`docs/coordination/USER_TODO.md`](../../docs/coordination/USER_TODO.md) | §A decision pool (`U-XXX`, **single source of truth**) + §B user-only work (API top-ups / concept-figure generation / reference research / strategic direction) + §C dispatch inbox (todos other roles assign to the user) |
| **Scientist** (writing lead) | [`docs/coordination/SCIENTIST_TODO.md`](../../docs/coordination/SCIENTIST_TODO.md) §B–E (§A is only a cross-reference back to USER_TODO) | Writing / figure design / Limitations tuning / framing decisions / paper-level lint / **reviewer-feedback handling (§B.4 S-104 four-step loop)** |
| **Engineer** | [`docs/coordination/implementation_log.md`](../../docs/coordination/implementation_log.md) | Append-only engineering log (run experiments / change core code / validate logs / post-process / add runtime guards) |
| **Reviewer** | [`docs/coordination/REVIEWER_TODO.md`](../../docs/coordination/REVIEWER_TODO.md) + per-batch directory `artifacts/idea_reviews/reviewer_<YYYYMMDD>_<HHMMSS>_<NN>_<6char-hash>/` + aggregated `scoreboard.md` / `fix_themes.md` | §A full-paper reviews (user-triggered only) + §B partial reviews (scientist / engineer may self-trigger to review their own newly written content); **stateless within a batch** (does not read past reviews when producing a new one, to avoid confirmation bias) |

Project-level single index (mandatory read for any role taking over a fresh window): [`PROJECT_STRUCTURE.md`](../../PROJECT_STRUCTURE.md).

## 1. Per-role operating procedure (CRITICAL)

Every role (user / scientist / engineer / reviewer) **must**, at the start of every new action, perform the following 2 steps. Both are required:

1. **pending → done sweep**: scan **the TODO file owned by the current role** for every ⏳ item or `等 <role> 完成 <ID>` (waiting-on) row, and check whether the upstream / external precondition has been satisfied. Mark satisfied items ✅ immediately and fill the "result link" column with a pointer to the produced artifact. This sweep is the entry point of the cycle.
2. **Update current-action TODO state**: when starting an action, add a ⏳ row in your own TODO; the moment it finishes, flip it to ✅ and fill the artifact link. If you dispatch new work to another role, also add a `tracking-XXX` row in your own TODO marked `等 <role> 完成 <ID>`.

**Skipping either step = work not finished.** The next role to take over will not be able to find the state.

## 2. "No nagging for the next step" protocol (HARD RULE)

When executing tasks:

- ✅ **Required**: complete **all** items in your TODO that do **not** depend on others before asking the user what to do next.
- ❌ **Forbidden**: pausing after every small task to ask "should I do A or B next?".
- ❌ **Forbidden**: handing the user options while there are still ❌ untouched and not-blocked items in your todos.

Hard rules for deciding "blocked":

- The task's `阻塞` (blocked-on) field is non-empty / points to an ID owned by another role and not yet ✅ → blocked.
- The task's `阻塞` field reads "user decision X" and `USER_TODO.md § A`'s row for that decision is still ⏳ → blocked.
- The task's `阻塞` field reads "user manual X" and the corresponding row in `USER_TODO.md § B/§C` is still ⏳ → blocked.
- Otherwise it is not blocked → start working immediately.

## 3. Cross-role coordination via the `阻塞` column

Every TODO file's `阻塞` (blocked-on) column must be explicit:

- Free-form: `等 <role> 完成 <ID>`, or `用户决策 <decision-ID>`, or `用户人工活 <U-XXX>`;
- After completing your own task, walk the other TODO files and clear the `阻塞` field on every downstream task that was waiting on you.

A typical four-role blocking chain looks like:

```
user (U-RES-001 top up the API)
   → engineer (run the fullval rerun)
   → scientist (S-009 replace the "pending" wording in §4.3 with real numbers)
   → reviewer (R-FULL-N — only the next full-paper batch can score the real fullval framing)
   → user (decide U-011 framing direction based on reviewer's overall score)
```

## 4. Decisions go through `USER_TODO.md § A` — never decide unilaterally

Scientist / engineer / reviewer are **strictly forbidden** from making the following kinds of decisions (illustrative, **non-exhaustive**, list of route-decision categories):

- **Structural**: choosing among a/b/c routes (e.g., where the LaTeX template lives, where rebuttal records go, how to merge dual drafts, directory restructures);
- **Freeze-related**: whether to lock the manuscript / submit to ARR / commit-to-EMNLP;
- **Budget-related**: whether to spend GPU cycles on a re-run / whether to top up an API quota / whether to switch backbone provider;
- **Scope expansion**: whether to add a new benchmark (e.g., MuSiQue) / a new ablation / a rebuttal-only experiment;
- **Reviewer trigger (full-paper only)**: whether to launch a new **full-paper** review batch (`R-FULL-XXX`) — partial reviews `R-PART-XXX` do **not** require user approval (scientist / engineer may self-trigger to review their own newly written content);
- **Version control**: whether to `git init` / force-push / squash historical commits.

**Every decision** is first added as a row `U-XXX` + "recommendation / awaiting user" in [`docs/coordination/USER_TODO.md`](../../docs/coordination/USER_TODO.md) `§ A` (the **authoritative source**); **at the same time** add a cross-reference row (only the ID + which downstream task it blocks + a status mirror) in `SCIENTIST_TODO.md § A` so the scientist can track their own blocked chain. **Do not proceed until the user replies in USER_TODO.**

Any **physical operation that only the user can perform** (API top-ups, generating a concept figure from a prompt, uploading the paper, registering accounts, OpenReview operations) goes into `USER_TODO.md § B/§C`.

### 4.1 Decision-dispatch dialog protocol (user instruction, 2026-04-20)

> **Source**: User instruction "需要我拍板的时候，你直接切到的 plan 模式，让我们选择就行" (R21).

When scientist or engineer needs to dispatch a fresh `U-XXX-decide` to the user, the following dispatch-dialog protocol applies (in addition to landing the row in `USER_TODO.md § A`):

1. **Switch mode**: invoke `SwitchMode` with `target_mode_id: "plan"` and a brief `explanation` naming the decision ID and why it requires user input. This makes the decision moment visually distinct and signals the user that a route choice is at hand.
2. **Render structured choices**: use `AskQuestion` to expose the candidate options (a) / (b) / (c)... as labeled radio choices. Each option's `label` should be one short sentence summarising the trade-off (≤ 80 chars), so the user can decide without re-reading the full `USER_TODO § A` row.
3. **Always include a "use scientist recommendation" or "skip / defer" escape**: even when the scientist has a clear recommendation, present at least one alternative + one defer option so the user is never visually cornered into a single answer.
4. **Wait for the user's `AskQuestion` reply** before flipping `USER_TODO § A` to ✅. The user's reply becomes the canonical decision; the scientist then writes a `§C done log` row + appends a `§D` revision row + dispatches downstream tickets per the standard four-role workflow.
5. **Do not use this protocol for retroactive decisions**: when the user has already volunteered an answer in plain text (e.g., user message "U-018-decide：a" or "U-020：继续跑"), skip the `SwitchMode` + `AskQuestion` step and treat the plain-text reply as the canonical decision. The protocol is for **new** dispatch moments, not for confirming decisions the user has already made.
6. **Exception — physical operations / `U-EXEC-XXX`**: physical-operation tickets (API top-ups / figure generation / SSH probes / OpenReview steps) go directly into `USER_TODO § B` as `U-EXEC-XXX` and do **not** require a plan-mode dialog (those are tasks, not decisions). Plan-mode + `AskQuestion` is reserved for genuine route-choice decisions.

This protocol does NOT replace the requirement that every decision still be persisted as a `U-XXX` row in `USER_TODO § A` with the full context, recommendation, impact, and status fields. The plan-mode dialog is the **dispatch UX layer**; the `USER_TODO § A` row is the **persistent record**.

### 4.2 Engineer-facing dispatch quality bar (user instruction, 2026-04-20)

> **Source**: User instruction "在你给工程师派任务的时候，把他的疑惑顺便给他解答了，放在这条todo的注意事项里面，让他知道刚才为什么失败" (R22).

When scientist (or any role) dispatches a new ticket to the engineer in `implementation_log.md`, the dispatch must **proactively pre-answer the engineer's known open questions / past confusions / past failure modes** inside the ticket's `pinned_cautions_to_acknowledge` field — so the engineer reading the ticket immediately understands "why this matters" without having to dig through unrelated history.

Required behaviors when writing a ticket:

1. **Scan engineer's recent open issues**: before writing a new ticket, grep `implementation_log.md` for the most recent ⚠ / partial-blocked / U-XXX-decide rows raised by the engineer; if any of those are now resolved (e.g., U-019 SSH ✅, U-020 fullval gate ✅), proactively **write a one-paragraph explanation in the new ticket's cautions** telling the engineer (a) what was happening, (b) why it failed, (c) how the resolution was reached, and (d) what the engineer should do differently next time.
2. **Cross-reference pinned cautions blocks for permanent reference**: if the resolution involves a recurring failure mode (e.g., SSH state pollution, provider drift, byte-id regression risk), don't inline the full explanation in every ticket — instead create a **single `[pinned_cautions_for_engineer_<topic>_<YYYYMMDD>]` phase block** that holds the full explanation + recovery playbook, then cross-ref it from each affected ticket's cautions. This avoids duplication and keeps the lookup canonical.
3. **Don't bury post-mortems in retrospective phase blocks alone**: if an engineer-facing failure was post-mortem'd (e.g., in a `[u_XXX_*_20260420]` resolution block), also retroactively add a **`#### 后补 ack (R<N>, <date>)`** sub-section to the original ⚠ phase block where the engineer first raised the issue, with: (a) status flip ⚠ → ✅, (b) one-paragraph plain-text explanation written **directly to the engineer in second person** ("你之前看到的 X 不是 Y, 而是 Z..."), (c) cross-ref to the resolution block + the pinned-cautions block, (d) explicit "下次再遇到一模一样的 X，按 [recovery playbook] 走 — 不要再挂 U-XXX-decide 阻塞用户". This makes the explanation visible to engineers re-reading the ticket they originally got stuck on.
4. **Tone**: write engineer-facing post-mortem text as **collegial, not corrective** — e.g., "**你看这一段**：U-XXX 用户拍板 (b)，scientist 直连 server 4 秒成功 ... 你之前看到的 `Permission denied` 不是 server 拒了你的 key, 而是本机 OpenSSH 客户端状态污染" — not "你之前的诊断是错的". The engineer correctly applied Rule 3 fail-fast in `ssh-server-rules.mdc`; it's the scientist's job to close the diagnostic loop, not to assign blame.
5. **What does NOT need this treatment**: tickets that are pure-greenfield (no prior engineer confusion to resolve), or tickets that are mechanical follow-ups in an established workflow (e.g., `validate_logs.py` invocation step in a routine fullval batch). Reserve the dispatch-quality treatment for tickets that touch a domain where the engineer has previously raised ⚠ / U-XXX-decide.

Example pattern that codifies this (from R22 commit):

- Engineer in `[E-010_chateval_server_clone_attempt_20260420]` raised `U-019-server-ssh-state-decide`
- User in R21 instructed "你去登录到远程看一下吧"; scientist in `[u_019_server_ssh_recovered_20260420]` verified SSH 4 s ✅ + 6-check diagnostic table
- In R22 (this rule's first application):
  - Created **`[pinned_cautions_for_engineer_ssh_failure_mode_20260420]`** as the canonical SSH-recovery reference (explanation + 5-step Recovery playbook)
  - Retroactively added `#### 后补 ack (R22, 2026-04-20)` sub-section to E-010 block (status ⚠ → ✅, plain-text engineer-facing explanation, cross-ref to pinned cautions)
  - Added **C-7 SSH failure-mode** caution + cross-ref to all currently-active server-using tickets (E-015, E-016, E-017)
  - This rule (§4.2) codifies the pattern so it auto-applies to all future engineer-facing dispatches.

## 5. Window-switch / new-conversation handoff protocol

When opening a fresh window, each role must read the following files first (≤ 100 lines per file):

| Role | Mandatory reads |
|------|-----------------|
| User | `USER_TODO.md § A` recent ⏳ items + `§ B/§ C` recent ⏳ items |
| Scientist | `SCIENTIST_TODO.md § B` recent ⏳ / ❌ items + `USER_TODO.md § A` (to see which decisions are blocking you) + `artifacts/idea_reviews/scoreboard.md` + `fix_themes.md` (latest reviewer state) + `PROJECT_STRUCTURE.md` |
| Engineer | `docs/coordination/implementation_log.md` recent ⏳ / ❌ items + `USER_TODO.md § A` (to see which decisions are blocking you) + `PROJECT_STRUCTURE.md` |
| Reviewer | `REVIEWER_TODO.md § A/§B` recent ⏳ items + the current paper PDF or partial fragment under review + `idea.md` (for framing context) + `prompts/reviewer_prompt.md` (system prompt); **do not read past reviews** (keep every batch independent) |

Compact handoff message (one line ≤ 80 chars per field):

```
Goal: <one sentence>
Success criteria: <verifiable artifact>
Relevant files: <≤ 5 paths>
Confirmed findings: <facts already established>
Open risks: <known blockers>
Next step: <one sentence>
Role: user / scientist / engineer / reviewer
Last TODO check: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM>
```

## 6. Minimum quality bar

- Every time you write a new `.md` / `.tex` / `.py`: after finishing, **run ReadLints on the files you just touched** and only commit if there are zero lint errors.
- Every time you run an experiment: **cross-check the numbers against existing ground truth** (when available); a diff > 1e-4 must be explained.
- Every time you change code: **preserve reproducibility** (script + seed + n_boot must appear in either the filename or the log).
- Every time you produce a review: **emit it in the structured format mandated by [`prompts/reviewer_prompt.md`](../../prompts/reviewer_prompt.md)** — free-form prose is not acceptable.

### 6.1 Experiment checkpoint-resume hard rule (user instruction, 2026-04-20 post-quota-incident)

After the `[quota_exhaustion_incident_20260419_2338]` (E-017 seed=42 lost ~9000 samples of F1=0 garbage before detection), the user issued a project-wide hard rule:

> **每次跑实验都留断点，要可以断点重跑而不是从头跑。**
> ("Every experiment run must checkpoint; resume-from-checkpoint MUST be supported — never rerun from scratch.")

This rule is now binding on **every LLM-calling or long-running experiment script** across the repo. Concretely:

**6.1.1 Per-sample checkpoint flush (runner-level, MANDATORY)**
- Every method runner must write a `_ckpt_preds.jsonl` one JSON line per completed sample, flushed to disk BEFORE the next sample starts.
- `runner.Runner.run()` (`workspace/idea04_core/runner.py:132-180`) already implements this; engineer must not introduce code paths that bypass it (e.g., batched flush, in-memory buffering with "flush at end").
- Any new method added to `METHOD_NAMES` must route through `Runner.run()` or replicate the per-sample flush contract.

**6.1.2 `--run-dir` resume interface (launcher-level, MANDATORY)**
- Every fullval / multi-seed / chain-XXX launcher script must expose a `--run-dir` argument that points to an existing run directory.
- Semantics: if `${run_dir}/_ckpt_preds.jsonl` exists AND `${run_dir}/metrics.json` does not exist, the runner resumes from that checkpoint (skips already-completed sample IDs; appends new ones).
- `scripts/run_e017_fullval_seed.py` (see `--run-dir` flag at line 67-72) is the canonical template. Cross-machine resume (local → server migration, per R36 `[E-017_migration_landed_ack_20260420]`) uses the same interface.

**6.1.3 Pre-flight quota / balance probe (launcher-level, STRONGLY RECOMMENDED)**
- Every script that issues > 100 LLM calls in one batch must call `workspace/tmp/newapi_quota_probe.sh` (or equivalent) before launching the batch; abort with clear error if status ≠ ACTIVE.
- For short scripts (< 100 calls), the probe is optional but best-practice.
- This is the specific guard missing in the quota-incident; its absence allowed the batch to burn through quota silently.

**6.1.4 Runtime fail-fast (runner-level, engineer E-020 ticket)**
- `llm_client.call_llm()` must detect HTTP 403 `insufficient_user_quota` (and sibling quota errors across providers) and raise a hard exception, NOT retry-and-return-empty.
- `runner.Runner.run()` must maintain a `consecutive_zero_F1_counter`; if counter ≥ 50 (configurable), halt the batch and emit an error log pointing to the quota probe.
- Pre-flight balance probe in every `run_XXX_fullval_seed.py` entry point before the inner runner loop starts.
- These guards are specified in engineer ticket **E-020** dispatched via `implementation_log.md` immediately after R37 quota-incident ack.

**6.1.5 Forensic preservation on checkpoint corruption (scientist / user / engineer)**
- If an experiment detects a corruption event (e.g., F1=0 for many samples, HTTP 403 flood), the **valid prefix** must be preserved (truncated copy kept in original run_dir) and the **corrupted portion** backed up to `artifacts/forensic/<incident_name>_<YYYYMMDD_HHMM>_incident/`.
- `workspace/tmp/rescue_valid_data.sh` is the canonical rescue script template; adapt per incident.

**6.1.6 Violation reporting**
- Any commit that introduces a new experiment script violating 6.1.1 / 6.1.2 / 6.1.4 blocks merge; reviewer or scientist rejects the commit in `implementation_log.md` with cross-ref to this rule.
- Exception: quick smoke-probes (< 20 samples, < 5 min total wall-time) may skip 6.1.3 but MUST still satisfy 6.1.1 + 6.1.2.

**6.1.7 Verification-by-grep hard rule (post-R42 amendment, R44 codified)**

Triggered by the `S-141 R26` real-regression discovery: R26 commit message claimed "Appendix C heading `(per Reviewer R-FULL-004 D5 request)` removed" but the commit's `edo_paper.tex` diff showed NO change to line 366. Six subsequent reviewer batches (R-FULL-006-A, R-FULL-006-B, R-FULL-007) missed this leak; R-FULL-008 P2 experiments-weighted (user: "特别关注论文的实验") incidentally caught it.

**Lesson permanently codified**:

1. **Every scientist self-exec `✅` ticket MUST be verified by an independent source-file grep before being marked `✅` in `SCIENTIST_TODO`**. "Commit message trust" is INSUFFICIENT evidence.
2. **All "heading / formula / specific-text deletion or replacement" class tickets MUST have an explicit grep-verification command written in the `SCIENTIST_TODO` acceptance criteria column**. Example for DR-5 class:
   ```
   Acceptance: `grep -n 'R-FULL\|per Reviewer' article/latex/edo_paper.tex` returns 0 matches
   ```
3. **After completing an `S-104` closure, scientist MUST run a "verification-by-grep sweep"** on a random sample of recent `✅` tickets that involved source-file edits — re-grep the asserted-removed/added strings and flag any discrepancy as a **real regression** (demote ✅ → 🟡 regressed, open new ticket for true fix).
4. **Applies recursively**: when reviewer-agent self-dispatches a ticket with "⚠ pending" marker in the status column, scientist closure MUST likewise verify by grep before marking `✅`.
5. **Scope of rule**: scientist self-exec tickets touching `article/latex/*.tex`, `prompts/*.md`, `docs/paper/*.md`, `docs/coordination/*.md` source files. Does NOT apply to pure TODO-state changes (e.g., demoting from blocked → active) or cross-reference-only tickets.

**Enforcement history**:
- 2026-04-20 R40: §6.1 rule codified after quota-incident; E-017 seed=42 resume (path (a) per U-Rollback-001 ✅ (a)) is the first experiment launched under this rule; E-020 engineer ticket dispatched to implement 6.1.4 guards; `workspace/tmp/newapi_quota_probe.sh` is the first concrete quota probe artifact.
- 2026-04-20 R42: `S-141` real regression (R26 false-✅) discovered; `edo_paper.tex:366` DR-5 leak actually removed in R42; **§6.1.7 drafted** as commit-message-lesson.
- 2026-04-20 R44: **§6.1.7 formally codified** as hard rule (this amendment); future scientist self-exec tickets required to pass verification-by-grep sweep before closure is accepted.

## 7. Failure-rollback protocol

Only commit-bearing roles (user / scientist / engineer) — the moment they notice that one of their own commits has introduced a regression (e.g., a new PDF Overfull warning / a numeric drift / a script crash / the paper crossing the 8-page limit / a build failure):

1. Immediately add a `U-Rollback-XXX` row in `USER_TODO.md § A` (and a cross-reference in `SCIENTIST_TODO.md § A`);
2. In your own TODO, demote the affected ✅ item back to ⏳ and append `⚠ regressed: <reason>`;
3. Wait for the user to decide between `git revert` / `amend` / forward-fix.

> **Reviewer-score fluctuation ≠ rollback trigger.** Small overall-score swings (±0.5 / ±1.0) between reviewer batches are reviewer sampling noise and **do not trigger rollback**. The scientist, in step ② "do not blindly follow" of S-104, **must weigh the historical score trend** (consult `artifacts/idea_reviews/scoreboard.md`'s cumulative distribution); a single small dip is not allowed to stall progress. Only when **multiple consecutive batches** point to a real regression on the same fix-theme should §7 rollback be considered.

## 8. Completion report and wait

After completing every actionable item in your TODO:

1. Send the user a concise status report;
2. Wait for the user's instruction;
3. While waiting, do not unilaterally launch additional experiments or new full-paper reviewer batches (partial batches are governed by §11.1).

## 9. Paper-figure division of labour

- **Statistical figures** (bar / box / line / scatter / heatmap — anything data-driven): **scientist** produces them in Python → `artifacts/figures/`, with a data-provenance md alongside.
- **Conceptual schematics / logical flow charts** (architecture / process / state machine / etc.): **user** draws them; the scientist **first writes a complete prompt** and drops it under `docs/paper/figures_prompts/` as a todo dispatched to the user (a row in `USER_TODO.md § B/§C`, mirrored by a `tracking-XXX` row in `SCIENTIST_TODO.md § B.3`).
- Any figure cited from LaTeX must have a `.pdf` or `.svg` vector version; bare `.png` is for in-document preview only.

## 10. Paper polishing → local commit

After every "paper polish" action (including, but not limited to: merging drafts, section-level rewrites, figure embedding, finalising numbers, copy-edit pass), **make one local commit**:

- Commit-message format: `<task-ID>: <one-sentence goal>`, body listing the affected files (≤ 6);
- Enables future revert / blame / pre-submission audit of the revision history.

Dependency: the repository must already have been `git init`-ed. If not, the commit-bearing tasks remain in `等用户决策 U-010` (waiting on the user's decision) state — do not initialise the repo unilaterally.

## 11. Reviewer role specification

> Reviewer is the **fourth** role in idea04 collaboration. It owns its own TODO file [`REVIEWER_TODO.md`](../../docs/coordination/REVIEWER_TODO.md) with two task families: **full-paper reviews** (`R-FULL-XXX`, user-triggered only) + **partial reviews** (`R-PART-XXX`, may be self-triggered by scientist or engineer).

### 11.1 Trigger-permission matrix

| Task type | Who may trigger | Purpose | Drop location |
|-----------|-----------------|---------|---------------|
| **Full-paper review** `R-FULL-XXX` | **User only** | Simulate an independent P5 oral-gatekeeper review of the whole PDF; pre-submission compliance pass before ARR / EMNLP | `artifacts/idea_reviews/reviewer_<TS>_<NN>_<HASH>/` (full batch) |
| **Partial review** `R-PART-XXX` | **Scientist / engineer** (self-trigger) | Review newly written / modified content (e.g., a fresh §3.7 rewrite, a newly embedded figure, a new ablation paragraph) | `artifacts/idea_reviews/reviewer_<TS>_partial_<HASH>/` (lightweight) |

**Red line**: scientist / engineer **must not** unilaterally launch a **full-paper** batch (covered by §4 red line). **Partial** batches may be self-triggered, but a tracking row must be added in `REVIEWER_TODO.md § B` (capturing what was reviewed / why it was triggered / artifact path).

### 11.2 Input / output contract

The complete schema, field definitions, and required fields are **all defined in [`prompts/reviewer_prompt.md`](../../prompts/reviewer_prompt.md)**, which is the single source of truth (to avoid double-source drift). This rule does not restate the field details.

The detailed I/O contract lives in `REVIEWER_TODO.md § E`.

### 11.3 Aggregated state (auto-generated)

- `artifacts/idea_reviews/scoreboard.md` — cumulative average / `latest_overall` / score distribution across all historical batches.
- `artifacts/idea_reviews/fix_themes.md` — deduplicated fix-themes aggregated across batches (the open feedback set).
- `artifacts/idea_reviews/review_index.jsonl` — metadata index of every batch.

Aggregation scripts: `scripts/summarize_idea_reviews.py` + `scripts/review_scoreboard.py`, triggered by the scientist after every new batch lands on disk.

### 11.4 Hand-off: the scientist's mandatory S-104 loop

Once a batch lands, **the scientist must immediately** execute the four-step evaluation loop defined in `SCIENTIST_TODO.md § B.4 S-104`:

1. **Read through** every key field of every review (the exact field set is defined by `prompts/reviewer_prompt.md`);
2. **Do not blindly follow** — judge each comment one by one ("does this point have real value?"); **must consider the historical score trend** (consult `scoreboard.md`'s cumulative distribution); a single small score dip is not allowed to stall progress;
3. **Honest acceptance** — comments that pass steps ① and ② are added to `SCIENTIST_TODO.md § C` (feedback-tracking table) plus a corresponding `S-XXX` fix-todo in `§ B.5`;
4. **Dissent log** — write down "why I rejected this comment" at the end of `§ C`, preserving an auditable dissent trail.

S-104 is a **permanent mandatory** item that is never closed with ✅ — every new batch re-triggers it.

### 11.5 What the reviewer does not do

- ❌ Do not read past reviews from inside a batch (preserves batch independence and avoids confirmation bias; `REVIEWER_TODO.md` itself only tracks batch metadata, not review content).
- ❌ Do not modify `USER_TODO.md` / `SCIENTIST_TODO.md` / `implementation_log.md` (output goes only to `REVIEWER_TODO.md` + `artifacts/idea_reviews/`).
- ❌ Do not unilaterally launch a **full-paper** batch (must be approved by the user; see §11.1).
- ❌ Do not participate in `§ A` decisions (the reviewer can only suggest fixes inside a review; decision authority remains with the user).

## 12. ID naming conventions (cheat sheet)

**Per-role prefix assignment** (user instruction, 2026-04-19): `U-x` user / `S-x` scientist / `E-x` engineer / `R-x` reviewer. Full inventory:

| ID prefix | Owning TODO file | Meaning | Example |
|-----------|------------------|---------|---------|
| `U-XXX` | `USER_TODO.md § A` (authoritative) + `SCIENTIST_TODO.md § A` (cross-ref) | User-owned route decisions / structural / budget / scope expansion / freeze / version control | `U-003` (dual-draft merge) / `U-011` (framing direction) |
| `U-RES-XXX` | `USER_TODO.md § B.1` | User-only: API / provider / resource ops | `U-RES-001` (top up kuaipao.ai) |
| `U-FIG-XXX` | `USER_TODO.md § B.2` | User-only: concept-figure drawing | `U-FIG-001` (Figure 1) |
| `U-REF-XXX` | `USER_TODO.md § B.3` | User-only: reference-literature curation (domain memory) | `U-REF-001` |
| `U-DIR-XXX` | `USER_TODO.md § B.4` | User-only: ongoing strategic / framing direction | `U-DIR-001` |
| `U-Rollback-XXX` | `USER_TODO.md § A` + `SCIENTIST_TODO.md § A` (cross-ref) | Rollback decision raised by any commit-bearing role after spotting a regression (see §7) | `U-Rollback-001` |
| `C-XXX` | `USER_TODO.md § C` | Concrete todos **dispatched to the user** by scientist / engineer (dispatch inbox) | `C-001` (dispatched to USER: produce Figure 1) |
| `S-XXX` | `SCIENTIST_TODO.md § B` (sub-grouped B.1 / B.2 / B.3 / B.4 / B.5) | Scientist's own writing / hygiene / fix-todo items | `S-001` (dual-draft merge) / `S-104` (reviewer-feedback loop) |
| `E-XXX` | `docs/coordination/implementation_log.md` | Engineer work item (this prefix is used for new phase blocks; historical phase blocks `[<descriptive-name>]` are not retroactively rewritten) | `E-001` (illustrative) |
| `R-FULL-XXX` | `REVIEWER_TODO.md § A` | Reviewer **full-paper** task (user-triggered only) | `R-FULL-001` |
| `R-PART-XXX` | `REVIEWER_TODO.md § B` | Reviewer **partial** task (scientist / engineer may self-trigger) | `R-PART-001` |
| `tracking-XXX` | "Dispatch-tracking row" appearing in any role's TODO | After dispatching work to someone else, leave a row in your own TODO that says "waiting on X to complete Y" | `tracking-S-009` (scientist tracking the engineer's run) / `tracking-C-001` |

**ID-numbering convention**:

- The numeric suffix increments **globally per prefix** (`U-001` / `U-002` / ...; the section a row lives in does not reset the counter).
- Once an ID is assigned, **never reuse it** (even if the task is cancelled, keep the row marked `❌ cancelled` for historical traceability).
- Before allocating a new ID, run `Grep "<prefix>-"` to find the current maximum and add 1.
